     19. COMMON CONVERSION MISTAKES
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="common-mistakes">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Avoidable Blunders</div>
    <h2>Common Conversion Mistakes</h2>
    <p>
      Several errors occur when making manual time calculations between GMT and Eastern Time. The most frequent error is **confusing EST with EDT**. Project coordinators often assume the offset remains a constant 5 hours year-round, forgetting that the gap drops to 4 hours in the summer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Another mistake is **assuming the UK and the US change clocks on the same day**. Because their transitions occur weeks apart, there are transition windows in spring and autumn where the difference changes, leading to scheduling discrepancies if ignored. Finally, people often make simple **addition or subtraction errors** (e.g., adding hours instead of subtracting when converting from GMT to Eastern Time).
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     20. WHEN GMT BECOMES EDT
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="gmt-to-edt">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Summer Shift</div>
    <h2>When GMT Becomes EDT</h2>
    <p>
      When Eastern Standard Time (EST) shifts to Eastern Daylight Time (EDT) on the second Sunday of March, the difference between Greenwich Mean Time and Eastern Time shifts from 5 hours to 4 hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      This shift affects standard scheduling intervals:
    </p>
    <ul style="margin: 16px 0 0; padding-left: 1.5rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      <li><strong>Standard Time (Winter)</strong>: 12:00 PM GMT is 7:00 AM EST.</li>
      <li><strong>Daylight Time (Summer)</strong>: 12:00 PM GMT is 8:00 AM EDT.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This transition remains active until the first Sunday of November, when clocks fall back to EST, returning the offset to 5 hours.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

